1
00:00:00,260 --> 00:00:02,662
BOAT, RAIN, MEAT...

2
00:00:02,755 --> 00:00:09,200
I've hesitated in front of easy words like these too.

3
00:00:09,350 --> 00:00:14,232
When two vowels sit side by side, do I read them both,

4
00:00:14,307 --> 00:00:19,360
or just one? I didn't even know if there was a rule.

5
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:25,690
Today, just one rule, and you'll read words like these instantly, without stopping.

6
00:00:26,292 --> 00:00:30,320
Hello, this is "English Made Easy."

7
00:00:30,471 --> 00:00:34,014
Today, when two vowels sit side by side,

8
00:00:34,090 --> 00:00:39,570
I'll show you the one rule for which one you read and which one you skip.

9
00:00:40,100 --> 00:00:44,168
Look at BOAT — the vowels are O and A,

10
00:00:44,262 --> 00:00:47,178
sitting right next to each other.

11
00:00:47,256 --> 00:00:50,840
Which one of these two should you actually say?

12
00:00:50,977 --> 00:00:54,404
Just O? Just A?

13
00:00:54,544 --> 00:00:56,690
Or both?

14
00:00:57,262 --> 00:00:59,440
Let me give you the answer first.

15
00:00:59,590 --> 00:01:06,683
When two vowels sit together, you don't read both — you say only the name of the first vowel,

16
00:01:06,766 --> 00:01:11,760
and the second vowel stays completely silent.

17
00:01:11,977 --> 00:01:17,480
In BOAT, you just say the letter name O, "oh,"

18
00:01:17,560 --> 00:01:20,120
and A makes no sound at all.

19
00:01:20,252 --> 00:01:24,480
Before I studied this, I thought you read both vowels too.

20
00:01:24,617 --> 00:01:29,350
Then when I actually heard the real pronunciation, it didn't match what I expected,

21
00:01:29,421 --> 00:01:29,934
"Huh...

22
00:01:29,984 --> 00:01:33,080
why is it like this," I'd think, and just move on.

23
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:37,730
But once I studied it, I realized you only need to pay attention to the first letter.

24
00:01:38,370 --> 00:01:40,260
The principle is simple.

25
00:01:40,425 --> 00:01:50,330
When two vowels stick together, instead of making two different sounds, your tongue just says the first vowel's name clearly,

26
00:01:50,399 --> 00:01:54,160
and lets the second vowel rest — it's just easier that way.

27
00:01:54,313 --> 00:01:57,680
That's why the second vowel makes no sound.

28
00:01:57,812 --> 00:02:00,320
COAT works the same way.

29
00:02:00,503 --> 00:02:06,496
O and A sit together, and O says its own name, "oh,"

30
00:02:06,592 --> 00:02:08,700
while A quietly rests.

31
00:02:08,898 --> 00:02:14,455
That's why it's read as "coat" — and actually, you already

32
00:02:14,526 --> 00:02:15,370
knew this word.

33
00:02:16,457 --> 00:02:22,360
Let's see how this rule works with words we already use all the time.

34
00:02:22,517 --> 00:02:27,840
RAIN from before — it's just A's name, "rain."

35
00:02:27,922 --> 00:02:31,540
MEAT is E's name too, "meat."

36
00:02:31,676 --> 00:02:40,340
Same with PAINT, "paint," and TOAST, "toast" — let's look at a few more.

37
00:02:40,428 --> 00:02:48,080
TEAM is E's name, "team," and PIE is I's name, "pie."

38
00:02:48,216 --> 00:02:55,370
Just remember the first vowel's name, and you'll read new words instantly, without hesitating.

39
00:02:55,996 --> 00:02:58,800
There's just one thing you should know.

40
00:02:58,982 --> 00:03:06,840
Today's rule works most of the time, but EA sometimes reads differently.

41
00:03:06,965 --> 00:03:13,254
HEAD has E and A together, but it's not "heed" — it's "head,"

42
00:03:13,369 --> 00:03:17,140
BREAD is "bread," DEAD is "dead."

43
00:03:17,290 --> 00:03:26,903
For these, you just have to get used to them one by one — today's rule is the starting point that unlocks most words,

44
00:03:26,966 --> 00:03:30,490
just remember it's not an absolute rule with zero exceptions.

45
00:03:31,219 --> 00:03:33,541
Now try it yourself.

46
00:03:33,720 --> 00:03:36,936
First word — how would you read it?

47
00:03:37,115 --> 00:03:39,080
Take a moment to think.

48
00:03:39,264 --> 00:03:42,939
The first vowel's name, exactly — it's "seat."

49
00:03:43,122 --> 00:03:44,951
And the second word?

50
00:03:45,125 --> 00:03:48,592
Same rule — it's "goal."

51
00:03:48,757 --> 00:03:52,720
Last question — this one might be a little different.

52
00:03:52,938 --> 00:03:55,122
The third word?

53
00:03:55,340 --> 00:03:57,960
The answer is "sweat."

54
00:03:58,135 --> 00:04:00,410
It's EA — the exception we just learned.

55
00:04:00,560 --> 00:04:05,120
Now let's check it in real sentences. First one.

56
00:04:05,420 --> 00:04:07,740
I need a new coat for winter.

57
00:04:08,240 --> 00:04:09,520
Second.

58
00:04:09,770 --> 00:04:12,170
It might rain tomorrow.

59
00:04:12,670 --> 00:04:14,150
Third.

60
00:04:14,400 --> 00:04:16,880
We had a team meeting today.

61
00:04:17,380 --> 00:04:18,700
Fourth.

62
00:04:18,950 --> 00:04:20,910
My head hurts a little.

63
00:04:21,510 --> 00:04:28,950
How was that? Now, even with unfamiliar words, just recall the first vowel's name and you can read confidently.

64
00:04:29,462 --> 00:04:33,070
Now, try saying it out loud yourself.

65
00:04:33,234 --> 00:04:37,510
Just repeat after me, and confidence will follow naturally.

66
00:04:37,731 --> 00:04:40,409
BOAT, slowly.

67
00:04:40,459 --> 00:04:45,179
Bo... t — put it together, boat!

68
00:04:45,477 --> 00:04:48,890
One more time, a bit faster.

69
00:04:49,118 --> 00:04:50,710
Boat, boat.

70
00:04:50,988 --> 00:04:53,490
Now it's MEAT.

71
00:04:53,824 --> 00:04:57,092
Mee... t — put it together, meat!

72
00:04:57,159 --> 00:05:01,995
One more time, a bit faster.

73
00:05:02,162 --> 00:05:03,830
Meat, meat.

74
00:05:03,988 --> 00:05:07,470
Lastly, let's look at the exception word too.

75
00:05:07,628 --> 00:05:09,050
It's HEAD.

76
00:05:09,432 --> 00:05:13,540
He... d — put it together, head!

77
00:05:13,831 --> 00:05:16,743
One more time, a bit faster.

78
00:05:16,844 --> 00:05:17,480
Head, head.

79
00:05:18,117 --> 00:05:23,690
Now, with just this one rule, you can read even words you've never seen instantly.

80
00:05:23,833 --> 00:05:26,970
Take this word, for example — you're seeing it for the first time today, right?

81
00:05:27,112 --> 00:05:28,827
Let's try it anyway.

82
00:05:28,986 --> 00:05:33,250
The first vowel's name, exactly — the answer is "grain."

83
00:05:33,422 --> 00:05:34,964
One more.

84
00:05:35,130 --> 00:05:38,810
Same rule again — it's "foam."

85
00:05:38,990 --> 00:05:40,250
Just one more.

86
00:05:40,439 --> 00:05:43,232
Same again — it's "reach."

87
00:05:43,303 --> 00:05:47,650
See? Even unfamiliar words keep reading themselves instantly, right?

88
00:05:47,804 --> 00:05:52,039
All this time, not knowing whether to read both letters or just one,

89
00:05:52,109 --> 00:05:57,600
not even knowing if there was a rule — it turns out knowing just this one rule was all it took.

90
00:05:58,207 --> 00:06:07,482
Today we covered the principle that when two vowels sit together, the first says its own name and the second stays silent,

91
00:06:07,574 --> 00:06:13,470
and also the exception where EA sometimes reads differently.

92
00:06:13,624 --> 00:06:17,253
Now, even when you see an unfamiliar word with two vowels together,

93
00:06:17,331 --> 00:06:22,890
you won't hesitate like before — you'll read it right away, without doubt.

94
00:06:23,046 --> 00:06:26,709
Next time too, I'll tackle another part you often find confusing,

95
00:06:26,787 --> 00:06:28,190
and unpack it together.

96
00:06:28,346 --> 00:06:31,850
If you keep learning one step at a time with me,

97
00:06:31,928 --> 00:06:35,510
before you know it, you'll be reading with much more confidence.

